There is no harm in polls, and they can be informative.  But since
the Development Team's attitude to polls is in question, let me clarify
a comment I made in a previous posting.

The Development Team is interested in IDEAS for changes, or in REASONS for
changes.  The mere fact that a group of people wants a certain change is
not enough (although it _is_ an interesting piece of information).  In the
final analysis we do what we believe is right for the game, and the only 
way to influence our actions is by influencing our beliefs.  In this respect,
it is possible for a single thoughtful posting to succeed in influencing us
to do something, in the face of an opposing poll.

A "poll" is one thing.  Calling it a "vote" or a "referendum" is another.
The latter presupposes a constitutional relation which does not exist, and
it creates undue expectations.  In doing so, it may disturb a wonderfully
creative, but informal, relation between the Team and the public, a relation
which has been the secret of our collective success.

-Izchak


